+/*
+ * Kick-only phase shared by resume-lab modes 2 and 3: send the wakeup
+ * IPI/SIPI (CPUHP_BP_KICK_AP) for some subset of frozen_cpus and return
+ * immediately. Each kicked AP parks at cpuhp_ap_sync_alive() until
+ * pm_finalize_cpu_resume() below releases it, so its hardware startup
+ * latency overlaps with whatever device-resume work runs next in
+ * suspend_enter() (Platform_wake onward).
+ *
+ * @primaries_only picks the subset:
+ *
+ *  false (mode 2, kept for comparison, NOT a shipping candidate): every
+ *    CPU in frozen_cpus, including SMT siblings of the same physical
+ *    core. arch/x86/kernel/smpboot.c's start_secondary() documents that
+ *    concurrent microcode loading on SMT siblings must be prohibited,
+ *    and load_ucode_ap() runs on every AP before it reaches the alive
+ *    gate this function's callers wait at -- so whenever the frozen set
+ *    contains a primary thread and its own sibling, this mode kicks both
+ *    of them concurrently. On this machine frozen_cpus is always exactly
+ *    {cpu1, cpu3} (core 0 never suspends; core 1 is a primary + its own
+ *    sibling), so every resume with this mode hits that case. It stays
+ *    in the lab so its timing/failure behavior can be measured side by
+ *    side with mode 3's, not because it is safe to run unattended.
+ *
+ *  true (mode 3): only the primary-thread subset of frozen_cpus.
+ *    Sibling threads are kicked later, in pm_finalize_cpu_resume(),
+ *    strictly after every primary in the frozen set has already reached
+ *    CPUHP_ONLINE -- the same primaries-then-secondaries ordering
+ *    cpuhp_bringup_cpus_parallel() already uses at boot (see above in
+ *    this file). No two SMT siblings are ever kicked while either one
+ *    could still be inside its microcode-sensitive window.
